      "body": "The Mamba2 ReplaySSM output-only kernel passes `input_precision=\"tf32x3\"` to `tl.dot` on every non-ROCm platform. `tf32x3` is CUDA-only: ROCm already falls back to its backend default via the `is_rocm()` guard (#49909), but other Triton backends receive a value they reject at kernel compile time (triton-ascend accepts only `ieee`/`hf32`), so the ReplaySSM decode path cannot compile on those platforms at all.\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nThe current guard is an exclusion list with only ROCm opted out (#49909):\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndot_input_precision = None if current_platform.is_rocm() else \"tf32x3\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny other non-CUDA platform (e.g. Ascend NPU via triton-ascend) therefore receives `tf32x3` and fails at kernel compile time, so `--use-replayssm` cannot run there at all. This PR inverts the guard into a CUDA-only whitelist:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndot_input_precision = \"tf32x3\" if current_platform.is_cuda() else None\r\n```\r\n\r\n`is_cuda()` is true only for `PlatformEnum.CUDA` (`vllm/platforms/interface.py`), so ROCm keeps exactly the behavior #49909 established.\r\n\r\n## Claims\r\n\r\n- CUDA: unchanged — still `tf32x3`, bit-identical kernels.\r\n- ROCm: unchanged — still backend default (`None`), same as #49909.\r\n- Other Triton backends: receive `None` (backend default) instead of an unsupported value. On triton-ascend the default is `ieee`, i.e. full fp32 precision — `tf32x3` only approximates fp32.\r\n- The flag only `tl.dot`, in the flush branch of `_replayssm_output_only_kernel`; the non-flush path uses elementwise `tl.sum` and is unaffected by it.\r\n- Single call site: this file contains the only `tf32x3` usage under `vllm/model_executor/layers/mamba/`.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\nAscend NPU 910B3 validation: the legacy guard reproduces the tf32x3 compilation error in the flush tl.dot. With this PR, FP32 and BF16 ReplaySSM pass 9-step staggered-window oracle tests covering repeated flush, output, checkpoint state, and ring caches. Nemotron‑3‑Nano‑4B also completes 2×32-token ReplaySSM engine decode with the Triton Replay backend; all 64 greedy tokens match the dense baseline. Engine testing used a separate Ascend metadata compatibility shim because the current V1 runner does not yet populate replayssm_decode_base_cpu for ReplaySSM.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Details\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n-    # AMD Triton does not support tf32x3, so use its backend default. CUDA\r\n-    # retains tf32x3 to preserve fp32 parity with the elementwise baseline.\r\n-    dot_input_precision = None if current_platform.is_rocm() else \"tf32x3\"\r\n+    # tf32x3 is CUDA-only; other backends (ROCm, Ascend NPU, XPU) use their\r\n+    # backend default. CUDA retains tf32x3 to preserve fp32 parity with the\r\n+    # elementwise baseline.\r\n+    dot_input_precision = \"tf32x3\" if current_platform.is_cuda() else None\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe change sits in the ops layer and is runner-agnostic; it does not change which runners upstream supports for this path.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\r\n\r\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\r\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\r\n\r\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\r\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\r\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\r\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n",
